</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qianqian Xu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:%20xuqianqian@ict.ac.cn"> xuqianqian@ict.ac.cn</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id13" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">IIP, ICT, CAS</span>, <span id="id14" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_state">Beijing</span>, <span id="id15" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">China</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qingming Huang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:%20qmhuang@ucas.ac.cn"> qmhuang@ucas.ac.cn</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id16" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution"> SCST, UCAS
<br class="ltx_break">IIP, ICT, CAS
<br class="ltx_break">BDKM, CAS</span>, <span id="id17" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_state">Beijing</span>, <span id="id18" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">China</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to their powerful image generation capabilities, diffusion-based adversarial example generation methods through image editing are rapidly gaining popularity. However, due to reliance on the discriminative capability of the diffusion model, these diffusion-based methods often struggle to generalize beyond conventional image classification tasks, such as in Deepfake detection. Moreover, traditional strategies for enhancing adversarial example transferability are challenging to adapt to these methods. To address these challenges, we propose a unified framework that seamlessly incorporates traditional transferability enhancement strategies into diffusion model-based adversarial example generation via image editing, enabling their application across a wider range of downstream tasks. Our method won first place in the "1st Adversarial Attacks on Deepfake Detectors: A Challenge in the Era of AI-Generated Media" competition at ACM MM25, which validates the effectiveness of our approach.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Diffusion Model; Adversarial Examples; Deepfake Detection
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding authors.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep neural networks (DNNs), despite their superior performance across diverse tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prabhavalkar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, remain highly vulnerable to adversarial examples—inputs crafted by adding imperceptibly small perturbations that can cause models to make erroneous predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Szegedy et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini and Wagner, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madry et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Croce and Hein, 2020</a>)</cite>. Crucially, these adversarial perturbations frequently exhibit transferability: examples designed to mislead one model can often deceive other, potentially unknown models. This property, known as adversarial transferability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2018a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pei et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, significantly heightens practical security concerns for deployed machine learning systems. It enables practical black-box attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papernot et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maho et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, where an attacker can compromise models without access to their internal parameters or architectures. Therefore, advancing the understanding of how to generate highly transferable adversarial examples is fundamental to developing more robust DNNs. 
<br class="ltx_break">Traditional methods for enhancing the transferability of adversarial examples typically operate in the pixel space, and these approaches can be broadly categorized into five types. Gradient-based methods (e.g., MI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2018b</a>)</cite>, VMI-FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and He, 2021</a>)</cite>, NEF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) optimize perturbations through momentum and variance tuning, while input transformation techniques (e.g., DIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, SIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, TIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2019</a>)</cite>) apply stochastic augmentations to improve generalization. Advanced objective-based attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> exploit intermediate-layer features, neuron importance, or feature-space discrepancies to craft perturbations. Model-Specific attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> leverage architectural characteristics of surrogate models to amplify gradient propagation or selectively mask model parameters. Ensemble-based attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2022</a>)</cite> aggregate gradients or predictions from multiple surrogate models, which reduce bias toward any single model. However, the adversarial samples generated by the aforementioned methods exhibit limited stealthiness, making them perceptible to human observers.
<br class="ltx_break">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, owing to their powerful image generation capabilities, have been widely applied to numerous downstream tasks. Recent works have leveraged diffusion-based image editing techniques to construct adversarial examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, thereby enhancing their stealthiness. However, these methods’ frameworks are inherently incompatible with traditional adversarial transferability enhancement strategies and struggle to generalize beyond traditional classification tasks, significantly limiting their practical attack effectiveness in real-world scenarios.
To address these limitations, we propose a universal framework that is not only compatible with conventional adversarial transferability enhancement strategies but also applicable to arbitrary classification tasks. Specifically, to ensure that the edited image is as similar as possible to the original image, we first use DDIM inversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mokady et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to obtain the latent variables. We select the latent at a certain timestep as the update target while freezing the latents of previous timesteps, using the image description provided by the vision-language model as textual guidance during the image restoration process. When optimizing the selected latent, suitable adversarial transferability approaches can be randomly assembled from pools of traditional methods. Overall, our framework offers two key advantages:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Universal Compatibility</span> It seamlessly integrates traditional and enhanced adversarial transferability methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broad Applicability</span> Our framework generalizes to all kinds of downstream classification tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Traditional Methods</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient Optimization-based Attacks</span>  These methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2018b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and He, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> improve adversarial transferability by optimizing gradient computation through techniques that stabilize update directions and mitigate local optima convergence.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input Transformation-based Attacks</span> This category <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2019</a>)</cite> improves transferability by diversifying input patterns via stochastic transformations, making adversarial examples less dependent on specific model architectures.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Advanced Objective-based Attacks</span> These approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> exploit intermediate-layer features, neuron importance, or feature-space discrepancies to craft perturbations that are more transferable across models.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model-specific Attacks</span>  These methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> leverage the architectural characteristics of surrogate models to amplify gradient propagation or selectively mask model parameters.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ensemble-based Attacks</span>  By aggregating gradients or predictions from multiple surrogate models, ensemble methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2022</a>)</cite> reduce bias toward any single model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Diffusion-based Methods</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While traditional methods have demonstrated significant improvements in enhancing the transferability of adversarial examples, their dependence on pixel-level modifications results in generated samples with limited stealthiness, rendering them perceptible to human observers. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> proposes DiffAttack, the first method to leverage diffusion models for adversarial attacks by crafting perturbations in the latent space rather than pixel space, achieving both superior imperceptibility and enhanced transferability across black-box models. AdvDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces a novel approach to generating unrestricted adversarial examples by leveraging diffusion models’ reverse generation process, proposing two interpretable adversarial guidance techniques that inject target-class gradients while preserving generation quality. Diff-PGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2023</a>)</cite> introduces a diffusion-guided adversarial attack framework that leverages SDEdit purification during gradient optimization, enabling the generation of adversarial samples that maintain natural image distributions while achieving high attack success rates. StealthDiffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces a latent-space adversarial optimization framework that combines Latent Adversarial Optimization (LAO) with a Control-VAE module to preserve image consistency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminary</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since we aim to accurately reconstruct a given real image, we employ the deterministic DDIM sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t-1}=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t-1}}{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{z_{t}}+\left(\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t-1}}-1}-\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t}}-1}\right)\cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t-1}=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t-1}}{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{z_{t}}+\left(\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t-1}}-1}-\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t}}-1}\right)\cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models often operate in the image pixel space where <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sample of a real image. In our case, we use the popular and publicly available stable diffusion model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite> where the diffusion forward process is applied on a latent image encoding <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{0}}=E(\mathbf{x_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{0}}=E(\mathbf{x_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> and an image decoder is employed at the end of the diffusion backward process <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{0}}=D(\mathbf{z_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{0}}=D(\mathbf{z_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classifier-free guidance.</span>
One of the key challenges in text-guided generation is the amplification of the effect induced by the conditioned text. To this end, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans, [n. d.]</a>)</cite> have presented the classifier-free guidance technique, where the prediction is also performed unconditionally, which is then extrapolated with the conditioned prediction. More formally, let <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext='\mathbf{\varnothing}=\psi(``")' display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>`</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>`</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>"</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\varnothing}=\psi(``")</annotation></semantics></math> be the <span id="S3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">embedding</span> of a null text and let <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> be the guidance scale parameter, then the classifier-free guidance prediction is defined by:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}},\mathbf{\varnothing})=w\cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}})+(1-w)\ \cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\varnothing})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.555em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}},\mathbf{\varnothing})=w\cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}})+(1-w)\ \cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\varnothing}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DDIM inversion.</span>
A simple inversion technique was suggested for the DDIM sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, based on the assumption that the ODE process can be reversed in the limit of small steps:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{t+1}}=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t+1}}{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{z_{t}}+\left(\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t+1}}-1}-\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t}}-1}\right)\cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{t+1}}=\sqrt{\frac{\alpha_{t+1}}{\alpha_{t}}}\mathbf{z_{t}}+\left(\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t+1}}-1}-\sqrt{\frac{1}{\alpha_{t}}-1}\right)\cdot\epsilon_{\theta}(\mathbf{z_{t}},t,\mathbf{\mathcal{C}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In other words, the diffusion process is performed in the reverse direction, that is <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{0}}\rightarrow\mathbf{z_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐓</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{0}}\rightarrow\mathbf{z_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{T}}\rightarrow\mathbf{z_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐓</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{T}}\rightarrow\mathbf{z_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to be the encoding of the given real image.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.23676v1/images/main.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/199;" width="429" height="199" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Framework of our method. The core of our framework lies in optimizing the latent representations at intermediate time-step to construct adversarial examples, while maintaining compatibility with conventional strategies for enhancing transferability.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of adversarial attacks against deepfake detectors is to craft adversarial perturbations that modify deepfake images—making them undetectable as synthetic content by state-of-the-art detectors—while maintaining high perceptual similarity to the original images. Given deepfake images <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we hope that we can craft adversarial perturbation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to produce adversarial examples <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}}=\mathbf{x}+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}}=\mathbf{x}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math> such that the deepfake detector <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> misclassifies <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math> as real label and the perturbation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is imperceptible, maintaining high visual similarity between <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. This problem can be formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min\quad SSIM(\mathbf{x_{adv}},\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min\quad SSIM(\mathbf{x_{adv}},\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\quad f(\mathbf{x_{adv}})=y_{real}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\quad f(\mathbf{x_{adv}})=y_{real}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Our Framework</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We display in the Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ A Unified Framework for Stealthy Adversarial Generation via Latent Optimization and Transferability Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> the whole framework of our method, where we adopt the open-source stable diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite> that pretrained on extremely massive text-image pairs. Since adversarial attacks aim to fool the target model by perturbing the initial image, they can be approximated as a special kind of real image editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Couairon et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Inspired by recent diffusion editing approaches. our framework leverages the DDIM Inversion technology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mokady et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where the clean image is mapped back into the diffusion latent space by reversing the deterministic sampling process:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Inverse}(\mathbf{z_{t-1}})=\underbrace{\operatorname{Inverse}\circ\cdots\circ\operatorname{Inverse}}_{t}(\mathbf{z_{0}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Inverse</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>Inverse</mi><mo lspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>Inverse</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>t</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Inverse}(\mathbf{z_{t-1}})=\underbrace{\operatorname{Inverse}\circ\cdots\circ\operatorname{Inverse}}_{t}(\mathbf{z_{0}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=VAE-Encoder(\mathbf{x_{0}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=VAE-Encoder(\mathbf{x_{0}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inverse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inverse</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inverse}</annotation></semantics></math>(·) denotes the DDIM Inversion operation. We apply the inversion for several timesteps from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the initial image) to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A high-quality reconstruction of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be expected if we conduct the deterministic denoising process from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Different from existing image editing approaches proposed to modify text embeddings for image editing, we choose to directly modify the latent at smaller timesteps, which achieves image editing while preserving the original semantic information of the image as much as possible. We here directly perturb the latent as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{\mathbf{z_{t}}}{\arg\min}\mathcal{L}_{\text{attack}}:=J\left(\mathbf{x_{adv}},y_{true},f\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐳</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐭</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>attack</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\mathbf{z_{t}}}{\arg\min}\mathcal{L}_{\text{attack}}:=J\left(\mathbf{x_{adv}},y_{true},f\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We can get <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}}=VAE-Decoder(\mathbf{z_{0}^{\prime}})=\underbrace{\text{Denoise}\circ\cdots\circ\text{Denoise}}_{t}\left(\mathbf{z_{t}},\mathcal{C}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mtext>Denoise</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mtext>Denoise</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>t</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}}=VAE-Decoder(\mathbf{z_{0}^{\prime}})=\underbrace{\text{Denoise}\circ\cdots\circ\text{Denoise}}_{t}\left(\mathbf{z_{t}},\mathcal{C}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>(·) is the cross-entropy loss and Denoise(·) denotes the diffusion denoising process.
In addition to classification loss, other losses <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{others}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{others}</annotation></semantics></math> can also be enhanced to maintain image consistency (e.g., L1 loss) or to improve the transferability of adversarial examples. The final loss can be formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{attack}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{others}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{attack}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{others}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">To further enhance image consistency, we incorporate text guidance by utilizing a pre-trained vision-language model to describe the image content. The prompt "Describe this image" is used, and the resulting textual description serves as text guidance during the reverse process.
<br class="ltx_break">The complete algorithm flow can be referred to in <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.2. Our Framework ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ A Unified Framework for Stealthy Adversarial Generation via Latent Optimization and Transferability Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. As we can see, by decoupling the inverse image restoration process from adversarial attacks to the greatest extent, this allows our framework is compatible with almost all traditional strategies for enhancing adversarial example transferability, while not relying on the diffusion model’s own discriminative capability for images. This makes it applicable to arbitrary downstream classification tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Our Framework</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Synthetic face <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, Real face label <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{true}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{true}</annotation></semantics></math>, Perturbation radius <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, Classifier <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[f_{1},f_{2},...,f_{N}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[f_{1},f_{2},...,f_{N}]</annotation></semantics></math>, Step size <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, Qwen <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Adversarial face <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Iterations <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Text embedding <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext='\mathcal{C}=\psi(Q(x,"Describe\ this\ image"))' display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>"</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>"</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\psi(Q(x,"Describe\ this\ image"))</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{t}^{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{t}^{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>=DDIM-Inversion(<math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) via eq (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.2. Our Framework ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ A Unified Framework for Stealthy Adversarial Generation via Latent Optimization and Transferability Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Random initialization perturbation <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Get clean image <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{adv}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{adv}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> via eq (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2. Our Framework ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ A Unified Framework for Stealthy Adversarial Generation via Latent Optimization and Transferability Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              //<span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">Transform-based attack</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              Image augmentation <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x_{aug}^{i}}=\mathcal{T}(\mathbf{x_{adv}^{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐮𝐠</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐝𝐯</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x_{aug}^{i}}=\mathcal{T}(\mathbf{x_{adv}^{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              //<span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">Advanced loss function and model-related attack</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
              Calculate loss <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="loss_{n}=\mathcal{L}(f_{n},\mathbf{x_{aug}},y_{true})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>𝐚𝐮𝐠</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">loss_{n}=\mathcal{L}(f_{n},\mathbf{x_{aug}},y_{true})</annotation></semantics></math> via eq (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.2. Our Framework ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ A Unified Framework for Stealthy Adversarial Generation via Latent Optimization and Transferability Enhancement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              //<span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">Ensemble-based attack</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              Ensemble loss <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="loss=Ensemble(loss_{1},loss_{2},...,loss_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">loss=Ensemble(loss_{1},loss_{2},...,loss_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
              //<span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">Gradient-based attack</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
              Update <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{(i+1)}=\delta^{i}-\alpha\times\frac{\partial loss}{\partial\delta^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{(i+1)}=\delta^{i}-\alpha\times\frac{\partial loss}{\partial\delta^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
              Update <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{(i+1)}=Proj(\delta^{(i+1)},-\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{(i+1)}=Proj(\delta^{(i+1)},-\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
              Update <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z_{t}^{i+1}}=\mathbf{z_{t}}+\delta^{(i+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mi>𝐢</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>𝐭</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z_{t}^{i+1}}=\mathbf{z_{t}}+\delta^{(i+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Setting</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span> The AADD-2025 Challenge provides a comprehensive dataset designed to evaluate the robustness of deepfake detection systems against adversarial attacks. The entire dataset is divided into sixteen subsets, categorized along two axes: generation method (GAN-based or diffusion-based) and image resolution (high quality or low quality). Each category contains four distinct image generators.All images in the dataset are synthetic, generated by state-of-the-art GAN and diffusion models, and exhibit a broad range of visual styles and qualities. This diversity ensures a challenging environment for both detection and adversarial attack tasks.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metric</span> To quantitatively assess the effectiveness and imperceptibility of adversarial perturbations, the evaluation metric jointly considers two components: structural similarity and attack success rate. Specifically, each adversarial image is required to preserve high visual similarity to its original counterpart, measured by the Structural Similarity Index (SSIM), while simultaneously being misclassified as “real” by multiple deepfake detection systems.
Let <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th image in the deepfake test dataset, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}^{ADV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}^{ADV}</annotation></semantics></math> its corresponding adversarial version. Let <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{C_{1},C_{2},...,C_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{C_{1},C_{2},...,C_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the set of classifiers. The final score is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{C_{f}\in C}\sum_{k=1}^{N}SSIM(I_{k},I_{k}^{ADV})\cdot\left[C_{f}(I_{k}^{ADV})=LABEL_{real}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mi>S</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{C_{f}\in C}\sum_{k=1}^{N}SSIM(I_{k},I_{k}^{ADV})\cdot\left[C_{f}(I_{k}^{ADV})=LABEL_{real}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of test images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SSIM(I_{k},I_{k}^{ADV})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SSIM(I_{k},I_{k}^{ADV})</annotation></semantics></math> measures the perceptual similarity between the original and adversarial images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{f}(I_{k}^{ADV})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{f}(I_{k}^{ADV})</annotation></semantics></math> is the prediction of classifier on adversarial image.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LABEL_{real}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LABEL_{real}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground truth label for real images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\cdot\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\cdot\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function, which returns 1 if the condition is true, and 0 otherwise.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">A high final score indicates that the adversarial image successfully bypasses the detection system while remaining visually close to the original, thereby reflecting both attack success and imperceptibility. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation details</span>  To minimize content distortion when the attack succeeds, we adopt an attack strategy with a gradually increasing perturbation radius. The initial attack radius is set to 0.05, the final radius to 0.3, and the step size between iterations to 0.02. For the transform-based attack, we selected the following data augmentations from the third-party library Kornia:
RandomVerticalFlip, RandomHorizontalFlip, CenterCrop, RandomRotation90, RandomChannelDropout. For ensemble-based
attack, we combined the classification losses from different white-box classifiers by summing them with equal weights. For the advanced loss function, we additionally incorporated an L1 loss as a constraint to preserve image content consistency. We used MI-FGSM as our gradient-based attack. For the guidance scale parameter, we set <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1</annotation></semantics></math> as suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mokady et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for better editability and reconstruction. We set DDIM sampling steps is 20 and we optimized the latent representations for either the 1st or 2nd timestep.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Experimental Result</h3>

<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.23676v1/images/adv-ori-diff-v2.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/119;" width="215" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Visualization of the attack effectiveness of our method. </figcaption>
</figure>
